 ## Tutorial
 
@@ -33,6 +33,26 @@ $ cd pushrod
 $ go run -exec sudo . # We need root to access the raw USB devices
 $ npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --web # Connect via streamable HTTP at localhost:1337
 ```
+
+To use the custom Buildroot tree, first setup `local.mk` (use `local.mk.tpl` as a template), then build it for the K3 and QEMU:
+
+```shell
+# `ccache` is enabled; the first builds will be slow, but susequent builds will be much faster
+# Caches are shared between the QEMU build and the K3 build where possible
+$ make -C buildroot build
+$ make -C buildroot k3-build
+```
+
+Then, for QEMU, you can use the verification scripts to check whether everything works; the system should boot into Weston:
+
+```shell
+$ make -C buildroot boot-display
+$ vncviewer '[::1]:5900'
+```
+
+For the K3, use Pushrod to flash (using the instructions set in the MCP server); it should also boot into Weston.
+
+For further validation, there are various `(k3-)verify-*` and `demo-*` targets that test everything from boot to sysupdate to Kubernetes support to CRIU live migration. These are currently quite hacky and will need a bit of cleanup in the future, but do work today.
 
